      "fullContent": "VCs will make billions on Anthropic's IPO, but Amazon is set to make much (much) more | TechCrunch –:–:–:–\nBuy One Founder Summit Ticket, Get a Second for Free. Register now.\nDisrupt doors open Oct. 13. Get your pass and bring someone with you at 50% off. REGISTER NOW.\nClose\nSite Search Toggle\nMega Menu Toggle Topics\nLatest\nAI\nAmazon\nApps\nBiotech & Health\nClimate\nCloud Computing\nCommerce\nCrypto\nEnterprise\nEVs\nFintech\nFundraising\nGadgets\nGaming\nGoogle\nGovernment & Policy\nHardware\nInstagram\nLayoffs\nMedia & Entertainment\nMeta\nMicrosoft\nPrivacy\nRobotics\nSecurity\nSocial\nSpace\nStartups\nTikTok\nTransportation\nVenture\nMore from TechCrunch\nStaff\nEvents\nStartup Battlefield\nStrictlyVC\nNewsletters\nPodcasts\nVideos\nPartner Content\nTechCrunch Brand Studio\nContact Us\nImage Credits: Samuel Boivin/NurPhoto / Getty Images\nAI\nVCs will make billions on Anthropic’s IPO, but Amazon is set to make much (much) more\nMarina Temkin\n6:15 PM PDT · October 27, 2026\nMany investors stand to win big when Anthropic goes public. Stakes held by firms including Menlo Ventures, Sequoia, Sound Ventures, and Spark Capital are expected to return billions .\nYet according to Anthropic’s S-1 , none of those VC firms owns 5%, the threshold for being included in the principal stockholders’ table. That’s highly unusual. In a typical tech IPO, lead venture investors hold double-digit-percentage stakes in the startups they backed. The reason is Anthropic’s size. At its $965 billion private valuation, a 1% stake is worth nearly $10 billion, so, for example, Menlo’s roughly $14 billion position is only about 1.5% of the company, according to recently reported Bloomberg figures.\nWhile the venture firms may lack sizable percentage stakes, Anthropic sold large stakes to strategic partners, companies that are also its business partners. Indeed, Amazon is likely the single largest financial beneficiary of the IPO.\nAmazon first backed Anthropic in 2023 with a commitment to invest up to $4 billion in the AI company. Since then, Amazon has poured billions more into Anthropic. Instead of taking stock upfront, Amazon structured much of its investment as convertible notes, a type of debt that converts into shares upon an IPO or sale.\nAccording to the S-1, Amazon holds convertible notes with $6.2 billion in principal outstanding as of June 30. That is Anthropic’s entire debt load. But as Anthropic’s valuation soared across successive funding rounds, the shares those notes will convert into became far more valuable. Amazon’s own filings valued its note holdings at $97.9 billion as of June 30.\nAs the value of Amazon’s convertible notes surged, Anthropic had to record the growing value of what it owes on its books. It reported the adjustments on its income statement under “change in fair value of convertible notes, derivative liabilities, and SAFEs,” a line that totaled $72.4 billion for the first half of 2026. The charge is non-cash, meaning no money left the company.\nAmazon’s notes could convert into as many as 1.84 billion shares, according to the S-1. But a cap limits Amazon to 12% of Anthropic’s “partially diluted” share count, a figure that counts some shares not yet issued, such as stock options. Amazon can waive the cap. Notes that can’t convert will stay outstanding and convert in later quarters, subject to the same limit.\nBut the notes are only part of Amazon’s holdings. It further invested $10 billion in nonvoting preferred stock (a type of equity), in the second quarter, according to Amazon’s quarterly report for the period ended June 30. Amazon did this by investing $5 billion in each of Anthropic’s last two funding rounds. Combined with the $8 billion it invested in the notes from 2023 through 2025, that brings Amazon’s total investment to about $18 billion.\nBeyond its stock gains, Amazon will also continue to earn significant revenue from Anthropic, which has committed to spend at least $110 billion on AWS over the next 10 years.\nGoogle, too, is set for a major windfall from both the listing and its cloud computing deals with Anthropic. The search giant held about 14% of Anthropic as of early last year, according to court filings that were reported by The New York Times , and has invested more since, including $10 billion in April. (Google was likely diluted in subsequent and massive funding rounds, with PitchBook analyst Harrison Rolfes telling TechCrunch he estimates its current stake at closer to 5.6%.) It has also secured at least $114 billion in cloud commitments from the Anthropic through 2033.\nWhile VCs set for massive paydays have little reason to complain, the S-1 makes it clear that the biggest winners of Anthropic’s IPO may well be tech giants like Amazon, which backed a would-be rival early on and now sell it much of the computing power it runs on.\nThe S-1 itself warns that some of those cloud providers also build rival AI models and that they may not always want the same things that Anthropic does.\nTopics\nAI , Amazon , Anthropic , Google , IPO , Startups , Venture\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission . This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nMarina Temkin\nReporter, Venture\nMarina Temkin is a venture capital and startups reporter at TechCrunch. Prior to joining TechCrunch, she wrote about VC for PitchBook and Venture Capital Journal. Earlier in her career, Marina was a financial analyst and earned a CFA charterholder designation.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Marina by emailing [email protected] or via encrypted message at +1 347-683-3909 on Signal.\nView Bio\nNovember 4\nBoston\nBuy One Pass, Get a Second Free Cover more ground at TechCrunch’s ultimate founder bootcamp of the year by bringing your co-founder or partner for free Book before the BOGO deal expires\nRegister Now\nMost Popular\nMicrosoft offers buyouts to around 60,000 employees"
