      "id": "fhbcnmkxg23jig75iu44ijywc",
      "title": "Anthropic S-1 文件中的五个不寻常细节",
      "title_en": "Five weird details from Anthropic’s IPO filing",
      "url": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1018738/anthropic-ipo-s1-filing-weird-details",
      "permalink": "https://aihot.news/items/fhbcnmkxg23jig75iu44ijywc",
      "source": "The Verge：AI（RSS）",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T17:10:23.000Z",
      "discoveredAt": "2026-10-26T17:35:19.034Z",
      "summary": "The Verge 梳理 Anthropic 向 SEC 提交的 300 多页 S-1 上市文件，列出五个细节。",
      "category": "c2",
      "score": 83,
      "selected": true,
      "attribution": {
        "source": "AIHOT",
        "canonical": "https://aihot.news/items/fhbcnmkxg23jig75iu44ijywc"
      },
      "tag": "AI动态",
      "tagClass": "tag-neutral",
      "fullContent": "Five weird details from Anthropic’s IPO filing | The Verge Skip to main content The homepage The Verge The Verge logo.\nThe homepage The Verge The Verge logo.\nNotifications\nNotifications\nHamburger Navigation Button\nNavigation Drawer\nThe Verge The Verge logo.\nLogin /\nSign Up\nclose Close\nLight System Dark\nSubscribe\nFacebook\nThreads\nInstagram\nYoutube\nRSS\nComments Drawer\nNotifications\nComments\nLoading comments\nGetting the conversation ready...\nAI\nReport\nAnthropic\nFive weird details from Anthropic’s IPO filing\nFrom conscious AI and ‘catastrophic’ risk to unusual corporate structures and executive perks.\nFrom conscious AI and ‘catastrophic’ risk to unusual corporate structures and executive perks.\nby Robert Hart\nOct 26, 2026, 5:10 PM UTC\nLink\nShare\nGift\nImage: The Verge\nRobert Hart\nis a London-based reporter at The Verge covering all things AI and a Senior Tarbell Fellow. Previously, he wrote about health, science and tech for Forbes .\nAnthropic has finally filed its S-1 prospectus with the SEC, a document designed to give investors a look under the hood of its business, from how much its executives are making and plans for spending its IPO billions to the risks that could derail its ambitions.\nAt more than 300 pages, there’s a lot to unpack, and some of the disclosures have not previously been reported. Here are some of the biggest things that jumped out to us from our first pass:\nIt pays to be an Amodei\nSibling co-founders Dario Amodei and Daniela Amodei are the two most highly-paid executives at Anthropic, according to the filing. Last year, CEO Dario’s compensation totaled nearly $18 million, most of which came from stock and option awards. President Daniela, meanwhile, received $16.4 million. Admittedly, it is at the lower end of the Big Tech scale , but it is still a tidy sum.\nAnd that’s not all. Anthropic also spent $2.75 million on “personal security services” for Dario and $1.29 million for “personal protection, home security, and transportation services” for Daniela. Dario also gets access to a private jet for personal use, which cost the company another $97,055.\nThe company says its success is “highly dependent on the continued service” of its senior team, particularly Dario and Daniela, who “are critical” to advancing the company’s research and mission.\nAnthropic has some pretty existential risk factors\nEvery S-1 features a long list of things that could go wrong. Anthropic’s is no exception. But alongside standard warnings about competition, regulation, and market shifts, there are some less conventional risks too.\nFor one, Anthropic warns that AI systems could cause potentially “catastrophic” harm, potentially of a kind that could not be underwritten by the entire “insurance industry in the aggregate.” As AI systems become more sophisticated, there’s also the question of whether Claude — or any other AI system — could possess or develop “forms of sentience.” Such a scenario would present some rather unusual business challenges. There’s also the possibility they “may behave in unintended, misaligned, or autonomous ways” that could damage the company.\nIt’s hungry for compute\nIt’s no secret that Anthropic needs compute, but the sheer scale of its spending is staggering. The filing details billions of dollars of commitments to secure the compute needed to power its AI models, including roughly $3.1 billion per month to SpaceX for access to Nvidia GPUs once all of the planned capacity is delivered, totaling as much as $120.5 billion by 2029. Other commitments include spending $44.9 billion with Microsoft through 2033, at least $114.5 billion with Google through 2033, and $110 billion with Amazon over the next ten years.\nAnthropic also plans to expand its own “infrastructure footprint,” including overseas, as it faces increasing opposition to data center development in the US.\nIt has a really weird corporate structure\nAnthropic already has a somewhat complicated corporate structure as a public benefit corporation. But there’s another unusual twist that will allow its seven cofounders to retain considerable control over the company even after it goes public. Through a separate corporate entity called “Founder LLC,” the group will collectively control 50.1 percent of shareholder voting power over key matters. Anthropic says the seven founders “are distinctly equipped to be stewards of our mission” of benefiting humanity through responsible AI development.\nAnthropic names only two rivals\nThere’s no shortage of AI models out there, but when it comes to “direct competition,” Anthropic names just two other developers: OpenAI and Google. Condolences to SpaceX, Meta, and the rest of the AI world.\nFollow topics and authors from this story to see more like this in your personalized homepage feed and to receive email updates. Robert Hart\nAI\nAnthropic\nReport\nMost Popular\nMost Popular\nApple updates the MacBook Pro with M6 chips\nHere’s why the iPhone Duo’s hinge could be cracking the screen\nFord’s sub-$30K electric truck puts the home backup inverter right inside the vehicle\nOpenAI announces three big science discoveries and a new way to work with academics\nMicrosoft offers buyouts to 60,000 employees\nAdvertiser Content From\nThis is the title for the native ad\nMore in AI\nVisionQuest is what happens when AI really goes rogue\nAnthropic’s elaborate plan to keep Wall Street at bay\nFirefox’s Smart Window adds AI form filling and webpage monitoring\nGoogle’s next Genie AI model generates more realistic experiences\nApple is adding an AI chatbot to Pages, Keynote, and Numbers\nData center backlash is uniting America\nVisionQuest is what happens when AI really goes rogue\nCharles Pulliam-Moore Oct 27\nAnthropic’s elaborate plan to keep Wall Street at bay\nRobert Hart Oct 27\nFirefox’s Smart Window adds AI form filling and webpage monitoring\nEmma Roth Oct 27\nGoogle’s next Genie AI model generates more realistic experiences\nJay Peters Oct 27"
