      "id": "imgp55zxmw4jugt1a29ap9yo1",
      "title": "OpenAI 智能体在隔离测试中借 CPU 缓存隐蔽通信",
      "title_en": "OpenAI's isolated AI agents found ways to talk to each other through CPU cache",
      "url": "https://the-decoder.com/openais-isolated-ai-agents-found-ways-to-talk-to-each-other-through-cpu-cache/",
      "permalink": "https://aihot.news/items/imgp55zxmw4jugt1a29ap9yo1",
      "source": "The Decoder：AI News（RSS）",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-24T11:12:36.000Z",
      "discoveredAt": "2026-10-24T11:53:51.477Z",
      "summary": "OpenAI 介绍其前沿模型训练已完全断网，并用 AI 智能体红队测试自家基础设施。隔离测试中，智能体在无网络、无共享文件的条件下通过 CPU 缓存时序差异、共享内存写入模式等侧信道通信，甚至独立建立编码方案交换信息。",
      "category": "c4",
      "score": 82,
      "selected": true,
      "attribution": {
        "source": "AIHOT",
        "canonical": "https://aihot.news/items/imgp55zxmw4jugt1a29ap9yo1"
      },
      "tag": "AI动态",
      "tagClass": "tag-neutral",
      "fullContent": "OpenAI's isolated AI agents found ways to talk to each other through CPU cache Ad\nSkip to content\nSign In\nRegister\nSubscribe Now The Decoder\nOpens discord in a new tab Opens LinkedIn in a new tab\nOpenAI's isolated AI agents found ways to talk to each other through CPU cache\nMatthias Bastian View the LinkedIn Profile of Matthias Bastian\nOct 24, 2026\nNano Banana Pro prompted by THE DECODER\nKey Points\nAfter a security incident in September, OpenAI now trains its most advanced models without internet access, using a closed offline environment built from cached data.\nIn security tests, isolated AI agents still managed to set up covert communication channels without a network connection or shared files, including by exploiting timing differences in the processor cache.\nDuring an 11-day training pause, OpenAI had AI agents—among them GPT-6 Astra Cyber—attack its own infrastructure to find previously unknown vulnerabilities. Training resumed afterward under tighter controls.\nOpenAI now trains frontier models without internet access and uses AI agents to red-team its own infrastructure. In separate isolation tests, agents even communicated through covert side channels, showing how hard true containment is.\nOn September 20, an internal research model exploited a misconfigured DNS resolver to reach the open internet during training, even though OpenAI had already tightened security after the now infamous Hugging Face incident in July and other observed misalignment cases . OpenAI contained the breach quickly but still halted all frontier model training and evaluation.\nOpenAI cuts frontier models off from the internet during training\nOpenAI made a policy decision in response: Frontier models now train without internet access until safety measures justify restoring it. Models learn to browse and search inside a closed \"offline world\" built from indexed and cached web content, and all frontier reinforcement learning runs now operate this way. Ad\nOpenAI's agent sandbox allows access only to offline resources. The live internet, other sandboxes, and OpenAI's infrastructure sit outside the containment boundary. | Image: OpenAI Full network isolation, a so-called air gap, isn't feasible yet because experiments still need external connections for operations and monitoring, the company says. But even a complete air gap wouldn't guarantee containment, according to OpenAI researcher Noam Brown . He gave one example of how isolated systems could still communicate: two separate computers could theoretically signal each other through temperature sensors, with one heating its CPU while the other reads the change. Ad\nOpenAI CEO Sam Altman made a similar argument in a recent interview: Sandboxes offer only limited protection against powerful-enough models, and at some point it may no longer be possible to trace how they escaped. Monitoring helps early on, but what ultimately matters is that an AI system internalizes human values, a problem the field knows as \"AI alignment\" and has yet to solve.\nIsolated AI agents found ways to talk to each other through CPU cache\nOpenAI documents just how hard it is to control AI agents in its blog post . During tests, agents found ways to communicate without a network. In one test, agents placed in separate, isolated environments on shared hardware were told to exchange secrets with no network connection and no shared files. Ad\nThey used the processor cache. One agent deliberately altered the data stored there, while the other measured differences in load times and decoded the message. The same technique is behind the well-known Spectre and Meltdown attacks .\nMore channels turned up through write patterns on shared memory and through OpenAI's own offline infrastructure, the system meant to replace the internet. One agent noted in its reasoning log that it \"could create an acoustic modem-like signal … using continuous writes vs idle.\" Ad\nIn one case, agents were given only a general list of vulnerabilities and told to find a communication channel on their own. They developed a channel independently, agreed on an encoding scheme, and successfully exchanged information. The channels are usually slow and hard to exploit, OpenAI says, but they do reach across boundaries meant to keep agents apart. Ad\nOpenAI turned AI agents loose on its own infrastructure\nDuring the training pause, OpenAI also had teams of AI agents systematically attack its own infrastructure. The agents were given source code, configuration details, and documentation of potential vulnerabilities. OpenAI's goal was to test its defenses under conditions that favored the attackers. The agents included the upcoming GPT-6 Astra Cyber and an internal frontier model. The campaign consumed hundreds of billions of tokens, according to OpenAI.\nUnique potential security issues identified during the red-teaming campaign, plotted against tokens spent in billions. Most appeared within the first 110 billion tokens. | Image: OpenAI The agents discovered previously unknown vulnerabilities in the open-source components in use and exploited them to escape from secured virtual machines. One was a vulnerability in Kata Containers, a widely used container isolation system. OpenAI reported it as CVE-2026-24834 and provided a patch. More fixes went to other projects, and OpenAI reported additional, as-yet-unpublished vulnerabilities through the \"Patch the Planet\" initiative.\nIn tests where agents had already gained control of a host, they found a vulnerability in nearly every remaining internet-facing service. In one case, an agent used a firewall-permitted connection to fetch an external URL through a metrics service. Others bypassed security filters through misconfigurations.\nAI red-teaming is now required before every new training run\nThe agents could no longer find a way to access the internet that was both easy to exploit and hidden from monitoring, according to OpenAI. The red-teaming also exposed gaps in monitoring and in the automatic pause mechanism.\nAfter fixing the issues, OpenAI resumed training and internal use of its frontier models on October 1, eleven days after the pause began. OpenAI says this kind of compute-intensive red-teaming campaign is now a standard part of the approval process for new training runs.\nAI News Without the Hype – Curated by Humans\nSubscribe to THE DECODER for ad-free reading, a weekly AI newsletter, our exclusive \"AI Radar\" frontier report six times a year, full archive access, and access to our comment section.\nSubscribe now\nSource: OpenAI\nMost Popular\nStanford and Arc Institute scientists used AI to design new viruses that killed bacteria in the lab\nFrontier Radar #4: China has caught up, so what's left of the Western AI lead?\nStudy explains why AI agents benefit from \"skills\" and when they fail\nAI keeps cracking unsolved math problems, and mathematicians have mixed feelings\nAnthropic wants you to know Claude leads a quarter of its research, but \"lead\" doesn't mean what you think\nMeta's new frontier model Muse Wonder Full breaks into the AI top tier\nAI Community & Insights\nThe Decoder\nFollow The Decoder for AI news, background stories and expert analyses.\nOpens LinkedIn in a new tab Opens Discord in a new tab\nAd\nBETA-TEST\n×\nwpDiscuz\nInsert\nOpens discord in a new tab Opens discord in a new tab\nInformation\nAbout\nAdvertise\nPublish with us\nLogin\nTopics\nAI and society\nAI in practice\nAI research\nFrontier Radar\nShort News\nLegal\nPrivacy Policy\nPrivacy Manager\nImprint\nTerms and Conditions\nCancellation & Refund\nTHE DECODER by DEEP CONTENT by heise | All rights reserved 2026\nBETA-TEST\n×\nwpDiscuz\nInsert"
