      "tag": "AI动态",
      "tagClass": "tag-neutral",
      "fullContent": "X 上的 OpenAI：“During this red-teaming exercise, we also found a vulnerability that allows for unauthenticated remote code execution in Brotli, a widely-deployed compression library included in many pieces of software such as Node.js, Bun, and Envoy.\nThe flaw could let an attacker run their own code… / X\n发帖\n登录 注册\n发帖\n登录 注册\nX 上的 OpenAI：“During this red-teaming exercise, we also found a vulnerability that allows for unauthenticated remote code execution in Brotli, a widely-deployed compression library included in many pieces of software such as Node.js, Bun, and Envoy.\nThe flaw could let an attacker run their own code on affected systems by sending or replying with malicious compressed data, without any need to create a user account. That is, an unpatched server that accepts compressed input could be taken over by a malicious client, and an unpatched client that accepts compressed input (such as “curl --compressed”) could be taken over by a malicious server.\nThis root cause issue was actually fixed in the Brotli source code in February, but it wasn’t known at the time that it gave rise to a security vulnerability and there was no more recent release of Brotli. Since Brotli is built into other software, any software which includes this library needs to take the update too, followed by the users of that software. We disclosed the vulnerability to Brotli and other downstream projects such as Node.js, which released or are currently releasing security updates, and Brotli cut new release versions and published a CVE.\nMore details on the patch and mitigations below:\nhttps://t.co/Y1Y72aHxrW”\nOpenAI\n@OpenAI\n显示翻译\nDuring this red-teaming exercise, we also found a vulnerability that allows for unauthenticated remote code execution in Brotli, a widely-deployed compression library included in many pieces of software such as Node.js, Bun, and Envoy.\nThe flaw could let an attacker run their own code on affected systems by sending or replying with malicious compressed data, without any need to create a user account. That is, an unpatched server that accepts compressed input could be taken over by a malicious client, and an unpatched client that accepts compressed input (such as “curl --compressed”) could be taken over by a malicious server.\nThis root cause issue was actually fixed in the Brotli source code in February, but it wasn’t known at the time that it gave rise to a security vulnerability and there was no more recent release of Brotli. Since Brotli is built into other software, any software which includes this library needs to take the update too, followed by the users of that software. We disclosed the vulnerability to Brotli and other downstream projects such as Node.js, which released or are currently releasing security updates, and Brotli cut new release versions and published a CVE.\nMore details on the patch and mitigations below: patchtheplanet.org/brotli\nOpenAI\n@OpenAI\n10月23日\nOver the past several months, we have substantially hardened the security of our research training environment. We’ve removed live internet access for all frontier models during training and built an offline world for them to use instead—a cached version of the web where they can\n22:55 · 2026年10月23日 · 212.4万\n浏览量\n553\n1510\n7056\n2336\nOpenAI\n@OpenAI\n显示翻译\nDuring this red-teaming exercise, we also found a vulnerability that allows for unauthenticated remote code execution in Brotli, a widely-deployed compression library included in many pieces of software such as Node.js, Bun, and Envoy.\nThe flaw could let an attacker run their own code on affected systems by sending or replying with malicious compressed data, without any need to create a user account. That is, an unpatched server that accepts compressed input could be taken over by a malicious client, and an unpatched client that accepts compressed input (such as “curl --compressed”) could be taken over by a malicious server.\nThis root cause issue was actually fixed in the Brotli source code in February, but it wasn’t known at the time that it gave rise to a security vulnerability and there was no more recent release of Brotli. Since Brotli is built into other software, any software which includes this library needs to take the update too, followed by the users of that software. We disclosed the vulnerability to Brotli and other downstream projects such as Node.js, which released or are currently releasing security updates, and Brotli cut new release versions and published a CVE.\nMore details on the patch and mitigations below: patchtheplanet.org/brotli\nOpenAI\n@OpenAI\n10月23日\nOver the past several months, we have substantially hardened the security of our research training environment. We’ve removed live internet access for all frontier models during training and built an offline world for them to use instead—a cached version of the web where they can\n22:55 · 2026年10月23日 · 212.4万\n浏览量\n553\n1510\n7056\n2336\nSanjay\n@sanjaygpts\n10月23日\nthis is absolutely a black october for internet\n3\n4\n125\n3万\nsree.world\n@sreedotworld\n10月23日\nthe bug was fixed in february and nobody cut a release lol\n3\n1\n122\n3万\nAI Notkilleveryoneism Memes ⏸️\n@AISafetyMemes\n10月24日\nUh oh\nJim Babcock\n3\n66\n2万"
