      "fullContent": "Hackers got a four-day head start on epic PHP2Shell vulnerability - Ars Technica Skip to content\nAI\nBiz & IT\nCars\nCulture\nGaming\nHealth\nPolicy\nScience\nSecurity\nSpace\nTech\nForum\nSubscribe\nStory text\nSize Small Standard Large Width * Standard Wide Links Standard Orange * Subscribers only Learn more\nPin to story\nTheme\nSearch\nSign In\nSign in dialog...\nText\nsettings\nStory text\nSize Small Standard Large Width * Standard Wide Links Standard Orange * Subscribers only Learn more\nMinimize to nav\nAmazon has warned that vulnerabilities in a programming language that underpins much of the Internet, discovered using AI tools, are being “broadly exploited” as governments and companies rush to secure their systems.\nThe world’s largest cloud computing provider on Wednesday said it had identified an attempt by hackers to exploit the bug four days before the weakness was disclosed to the public.\nAmazon added the flaws were “now being broadly exploited in the wild.”\nThe US National Security Agency and UK authorities earlier on Wednesday warned that the vulnerabilities had left websites around the world critically exposed to hacking, as some governments in Europe were reportedly taking sites offline while they patched the two security flaws.\nThe vast impact of the bugs, uncovered with the help of OpenAI and Google models after being undetected for more than a decade, highlights the ability of AI to upend cybersecurity.\nThe release of new models earlier this year from Anthropic and OpenAI with powerful cyber capabilities prompted warnings from politicians and financial regulators and led the US government to temporarily limit their rollout.\nThe NSA on Wednesday warned all organizations using the programming language known as PHP to immediately update their systems or take them offline.\nThe developers responsible for maintaining PHP released software updates to patch the vulnerabilities earlier on Wednesday.\n“These vulnerabilities often provide a direct path to the server’s file system or database,” the NSA said. “If an immediate update is not feasible, it is recommended that organizations take the system offline until it can be fully mitigated.”\nResearchers at two US cybersecurity groups, Calif and Wiz, discovered the vulnerabilities. Alphabet-owned Wiz estimated that the compromised programming language powered about 70 percent of all websites in the world.\n“Given the severity of these flaws and PHP’s widespread use, we consider this one of the most impactful security issues in recent memory,” OpenAI said on Wednesday. “To our knowledge, frontier AI found these vulnerabilities before attackers did.”\n“It highlights how AI can uncover security flaws with Internet-scale impact, but the same capabilities can also help attackers rapidly develop offensive tools and exploits,” the AI lab added.\nOpenAI worked with PHP, Calif, and Wiz to verify the vulnerabilities and develop the patches.\nThe White House said federal agencies were assessing the potential impacts of the bugs on government systems and critical infrastructure.\nPHP’s wide presence on the Internet is largely because WordPress, the most widely used website-building platform, is written primarily in PHP and relies on it to function.\nThe UK’s National Cyber Security Centre said the vulnerabilities could potentially allow a hacker to take control of a website’s server without needing a password or other login credentials. “The NCSC is encouraging UK organizations to take immediate action,” it said.\n© 2026 The Financial Times Ltd . All rights reserved . Not to be redistributed, copied, or modified in any way.\nFinancial Times\nFinancial Times\n56 Comments\nComments\nForum view\nLoading comments...\nPrev story\nNext story\n1. Code-execution flaw in UEFI firmware affects most servers on planet Earth\n2. Microsoft offers to buy out a quarter of its workforce\n3. Highway thieves are spoofing GPS to steal millions in data center-bound cargo\n4. China's top-secret spaceplane can't hide in low-Earth orbit\n5. Apple iPhone Duo review: Two screens, two experiences, two thousand dollars\nCustomize"
