      "fullContent": "OpenAI’s Patch the Planet program is 1 of 2 parties to discover PHP2Shell vulnerabilities in core PHP affecting 70% of the web, using GPT-5.6 Sol; coordinated response over 2 months — Latent Space Skip to content\nSearch mode ? Search tips Keyword matches words; semantic matches meaning and exact titles or names; hybrid combines both. In keyword mode, use quotation marks for exact phrases. Author matches published credits. Year applies to posts; wiki entries have no author or publication year. You can also use author:\"Ada\" or type:podcast in non-regex searches.\nKeyword Semantic Hybrid Regex\nPublished Any year 2026 2025 2024 2023 2022 Author Reset filters\nExplore articles, conversations, and the wiki.\nTry a search: context engineering agents retrieval augmented generation\nOpen full search\nSubscribe Welcome back ⌄ Signed in with Google Email preferences Membership & billing Sign out\n← AI News AI News OpenAI’s Patch the Planet program is 1 of 2 parties to discover PHP2Shell vulnerabilities in core PHP affecting 70% of the web, using GPT-5.6 Sol; coordinated response over 2 months\nThe Big Hardening begins\nLatent Space Oct 22 · 37 min read\nShare\nAlmost 2 months ago, OpenAI introduced Daybreak for Frontline Defenders , a $1B subsidy for access to Daybreak cyber models and products. That funding came into use almost immediately, as major vulnerabilities in PHP were independently discovered by two parties: Ryan Austin, a researcher at the security firm Calif, as part of the OpenAI “ Patch the Planet ” project in August 2026 using GPT 5.6 Sol:\nView post on X\n… as well as Wiz, recently acquired into Google Cloud (using Gemini 4 Argon and GPT-5.6 Sol, as well as their own tool, Wiz Atlas).\nAll high profile cyber disclosures are at least a little bit of marketing, and the degree to which this was a severely alarming vulnerability on the order of famous ones like log4shell and Heartbleed is up to some debate , but surely the coordinated response across the PHP Ecosystem is the developer community responding in its most ideal form.\nWe should expect to be in for at least a year of finding such critical vulnerabilities; but it is the wrong move to blame the messenger for finding them; rather it is good to know that our critical software is generally getting the biggest security audit it has ever had in human history.\nAI News for 10/20/2026-10/21/2026. We checked 12 subreddits, 544 Twitters and no further Discords. AINews’ website lets you search all past issues. As a reminder, AINews is now a section of Latent Space . You can opt in/out of email frequencies!\nAI Twitter Recap\nTop Story: PHP2Shell—chronology, core claims, and the complications behind the emergency response\nWhat happened\nResearchers disclosed two PHP-engine memory-corruption vulnerabilities, with demonstrated unauthenticated exploitation against major CMSs and an unusually broad coordinated response.\nThe researchers named the vulnerabilities PHP2Shell and identified them as CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619. Their technical description places the defects in PHP’s internal handling of out-of-memory conditions and fatal errors—not in a WordPress plugin or an application’s authentication logic. They report demonstrations of full remote code execution and “Heartbleed-style” memory disclosure. @sagitz_\nThe disclosed demonstrations included pre-authentication RCE against WordPress, Drupal, and Joomla. The researcher’s announcement separately claims remote memory disclosure against any PHP application. These are researcher-reported exploit results; they should not be silently expanded into a claim that every deployment has an identical, immediately exploitable RCE path. @sagitz_\nOpenAI says Calif researchers used its models to discover the flaws, while secondary reporting says Wiz independently found the same bugs. Posts identify GPT-5.6 Sol on the Calif/OpenAI side; a Wiz-affiliated account credits Gemini 4 Argon with discovery, exploit-chain development, detector creation, and patch validation. This is a multi-party discovery story, not evidence that one vendor alone found and fixed the issue. @TheRundownAI , @steipete , @ralucaadapopa\nOpenAI describes approximately two months of coordinated remediation before disclosure. It says the patch required a major PHP-engine change and coordination among maintainers, researchers, security providers, and governments. A participating researcher confirms work with the PHP Foundation to support and harden the patch. @OpenAI , @itszn13\nCloudflare deployed initial WAF mitigations before the scheduled patch release. Its CTO said rules covered known exploit vectors, were available to all customers including the free tier, and were being updated as early attacks emerged. He explicitly called the rules a stopgap and directed operators to patch at 14:00 UTC . @dok2001\nThe supplied NSA account advised preparation for an immediate update—and taking systems offline if timely mitigation was not possible. Later OpenAI messaging said upgrades were available; secondary reporting says details leaked before the patch. The supplied material does not establish precisely what leaked, when, or whether the release schedule changed. @NSACyber , @OpenAI , @TheRundownAI\nReconstructing the chronology\n1. Discovery and private coordination: approximately two months before public disclosure. OpenAI places the work under Patch the Planet and says it led a coalition preparing fixes and protections. Its security researcher Eric Wallace adds that internal teams worked on patching and detection rather than merely disclosing the bug, with confidential advance notification to governments and outside agencies. Greg Brockman reiterates the two-month coordination period. These are participant accounts, not an independently audited incident timeline. @Eric_Wallace_ , @gdb\nThe distinction between program names matters: the posts associate the vulnerability with Patch the Planet , while researchers credit Daybreak model access for enabling security work. Those are related roles—remediation initiative versus access mechanism—not interchangeable descriptions of the same program. @OpenAI , @S1r1u5_\n2. Advance public warnings and a scheduled patch window. Japanese-language posts warned operators to prepare for a 23:00 JST, October 21 release, consistent with Cloudflare’s 14:00 UTC announcement. One correctly emphasized that a PHP-core vulnerability cannot be fixed merely by waiting for plugin developers. Another framed the event as a “15-minute” race after patch publication, but that number was a prediction—not a measured time to weaponization. @ds_nakajima , @expokun\n3. Pre-release filtering and observed attack activity. Cloudflare’s post is the strongest firsthand operational evidence in the supplied record: mitigations were already deployed, known vectors were covered, and “early attacks” were appearing. It does not specify successful compromises, request counts, victim counts, payload provenance, or whether the traffic represented original exploit development versus reuse of leaked information. @dok2001\n4. Research disclosure, government warning, and patch availability. The researcher’s public thread describes the exploit demonstrations and error-handling root cause. The NSA post calls for urgent preparation and conditional shutdown. OpenAI subsequently says every PHP site can upgrade safely. The supplied set contains no direct PHP Foundation release advisory, exact fixed-version matrix, distribution package identifiers, or patch commit. That missing primary-source layer prevents a reliable “upgrade to version X” instruction. @sagitz_ , @NSACyber , @OpenAI\n5. Alleged premature exposure and widespread service disruption. The Rundown reports that details leaked early. A later viral compilation adds approximately 1.5 million servers , attributed to Censys, running versions without a fix; reported shutdowns involving Dutch institutions, government sites elsewhere in Europe, Wikipedia, Archive.org, Asana, GOG, and others; and a 05:55 warning from Japan’s prime minister. None of those individual claims is corroborated by its underlying advisory, measurement, or status page in the supplied material. Nor does the record establish whether each outage was a precaution, a patching interruption, an unrelated failure, or a compromise. @TheRundownAI , @fiveoutofnine\nSemiAnalysis separately reports that Japan’s prime minister urged PHP operators to patch immediately or temporarily disconnect if they could not. Its account contextualizes that response with an existing Japanese breach wave, rather than establishing that PHP2Shell caused those earlier incidents. @SemiAnalysis_\n6. Exposure-checking assistance followed disclosure. OpenAI published a repository containing a Codex skill intended to guide operators through checking whether their sites were affected. That is an assistance workflow, not a substitute for a vendor advisory, package verification, or evidence that every vulnerable runtime has been replaced. @OpenAI\nAI’s role: credible evidence, competing credit, and unresolved causality\nThe affirmative case is stronger than a benchmark-only claim. Researchers describe working exploit chains, coordinated patch engineering, detectors, and patch validation. A participating researcher calls PHP2Shell the most consequential vulnerability they have helped discover. OpenAI characterizes it as an example of the “Defender’s Window” : advanced models finding a flaw before attackers, giving defenders time to prepare. Importantly, its wording is “to our knowledge” —not proof that no attacker previously knew the vulnerabilities. @itszn13 , @OpenAI\nCredit is distributed across humans, models, and infrastructure providers. Calif/OpenAI and Wiz/Gemini appear as separate discovery accounts; PHP maintainers supplied the upstream remediation path; Cloudflare supplied pre-release filtering. Google also advertises Wiz testing in which Gemini 4 Argon found more than twice as many vulnerabilities as baseline models , but the supplied promotional post does not expose the dataset, baselines, budgets, or false-positive accounting. It is not an independent comparative evaluation of PHP2Shell discovery performance. @TheRundownAI , @ralucaadapopa , @Google\nResearcher-access policy draws both praise and criticism. One researcher credits OpenAI’s Daybreak access with a portfolio of findings spanning PHP, GitLab, Chrome, WordPress, and other targets. Another sharply contrasts OpenAI’s vetted access with Anthropic’s restrictions; the claim that Anthropic contributed “zero value” is that author’s sweeping opinion, not established by the PHP record. A further post criticizes restrictions on Astra access and argues for giving 1,000 vetted researchers the best available models with intensive monitoring, rather than a rigid red/blue distinction. @S1r1u5_ , @migtissera , @S1r1u5_\nThe countervailing safety rationale appears in reporting that Anthropic delayed broad access to Mythos-class models for four months to strengthen cyber safeguards. That provides context for restricted access, but does not settle whether its implementation helped or hindered this particular remediation effort. @kimmonismus\nA burst of disclosures is not, by itself, evidence of a burst of AI-caused compromises. The supplied feed mixes defensive discovery, supply-chain attacks, credential theft, precautionary shutdowns, and unrelated vulnerabilities. Ethan Mollick interprets the moment as attack and defense accelerating beyond human response speeds; John Allard describes a capability “phase transition” while acknowledging that many recent findings came from white-hat programs. Both are interpretations of the pattern, not incident-level causal attribution. @emollick , @john__allard\nOperational complications: patching, shutdowns, and the temptation to “fix everything”\nThe emergency is as much distribution and ownership as discovery. Operators need to locate PHP across managed hosting, containers, appliances, background workers, and old applications; obtain appropriate packages; deploy them; restart or replace affected processes; and verify the running build. One operator describes maintaining numerous PHP/WordPress sites and being exhausted by testing and updates. Another concludes that patch distribution is poorly coordinated. @ostrisai , @jd_pressman\nWAF deployment reduces immediate exposure but creates a false-confidence risk. Cloudflare’s own statement is narrower than the later advice to put all internet-facing services behind it: known vectors were covered, protections were evolving, and patching remained mandatory. Proxied coverage, alternate ingress paths, and the origin’s exposure must be checked rather than assumed. @dok2001 , @threepointone\nShutdown guidance is conditional, not evidence that the entire internet should disconnect. The supplied NSA post recommends taking systems offline when immediate updating is infeasible until full mitigation is possible. For a specific operator, the relevant questions are affected-version status, exposure, available mitigations, and the consequences of continued operation—not the engagement score of an alarming post. @NSACyber\n“Use the RCE to auto-patch every server” is a proposal, not an authorized remediation plan. Marc Köhlbrugge raises the idea; J. D. Pressman suggests a presidential pardon for a lab or white-hat team attempting it. Unsolicited exploitation still risks unauthorized access, outages, incorrect fixes, destruction of evidence, and cross-jurisdictional liability. A speculative pardon does not provide universal authorization or eliminate civil and foreign legal exposure. @marckohlbrugge , @jd_pressman\nUpdate velocity has a supply-chain trade-off. One widely shared post captures the dilemma: updating constantly can expose users to compromised packages, while delaying updates leaves known vulnerabilities open. Pressman argues that endless patching is unsustainable unless the rate of new vulnerabilities and risky releases falls; he also notes that receiving no patch and discovering theft is worse than receiving too many updates. @xeophon , @jd_pressman , @jd_pressman\nThe longer-term alternatives remain open questions. Sarah Guo reports CVEs increasing from roughly 4,000 per month to 15,000 since April , and asks about formal verification, safer distributions, and organizations without red teams. The counts are not independently verified here, and disclosure volume is not a direct measure of successful exploitation. Other posts ask what a full-codebase security sweep would cost and recast the challenge as hardening mundane infrastructure rather than debating only abstract catastrophe. @saranormous , @saranormous , @teortaxesTex , @mimi10v3\nFor an incident team, the defensible sequence is to obtain the authoritative PHP and distribution advisories, inventory exposed runtimes, apply temporary protections where justified, deploy authenticated fixed packages, verify running processes, and investigate evidence of exploitation separately. Updating closes a vulnerability; it does not establish that credentials or data were never exposed.\nPublic reactions: practical caution versus unsupported escalation\nThe precautionary perspective stresses that interconnected services are fragile and remediation can be costly. Joshua Achiam compares the moment to March 2020 , speculates about internet lockdowns, recommends backing up valued internet material, and suggests printing important records. Pressman says remediation costs exceeded his expectations. These are preparedness judgments—not evidence of impending systemic collapse. Paper copies may help continuity but are not inherently tamperproof logs. @jachiam0 , @jachiam0 , @jachiam0 , @jd_pressman\nThe alarm-skeptical perspective asks for specificity. Alex Johnson argues that warnings about bank accounts being arbitrarily emptied obscure the distinction between unauthorized transfers and scams in which customers authorize payment. He invokes U.S. Electronic Funds Transfer Act protections with timely reporting; those protections are jurisdiction-, account-, and circumstance-dependent, not a universal guarantee. Kelsey Piper separately warns about the psychological cost of living under constant annihilation framing. @AlexH_Johnson , @KelseyTuoc\nBroad personal-security advice needs to be separated from the PHP fix. Password managers, unique credentials, strong MFA, patching, carrier port-out protections, and U.S.-specific credit or tax identity safeguards can reduce downstream harm. But enabling 2FA does not prevent exploitation of an unauthenticated PHP-engine bug. Likewise, viral advice to avoid all online payments the next day is not supported by a demonstrated scope assessment. @peterwildeford , @StillMare_\nThe broader threat context is real but not interchangeable with PHP2Shell. SemiAnalysis reports 18 Japanese companies disclosing breaches since September 25, including exposures of approximately 6.6 million Times Car accounts , 11.18 million Burger King Japan app records , and potentially 22.5 million Sourcenext records . It attributes findings about AI-assisted scanning and API attacks to JPCERT/CC. Another post quotes an August 19 advisory concerning AI-assisted tooling against internet-exposed Siemens PLCs. These are separate reported incidents and advisories; neither establishes PHP2Shell as their entry point. @SemiAnalysis_ , @AndrewCurran_\nThe key unresolved evidence gaps are concrete: exact affected and fixed versions; CVSS vectors; deployment prerequisites; the independent discovery timeline; what leaked before release; successful exploitation versus scanning; validated exposure counts; and the cause and duration of each reported shutdown. The supplied viral incident compilation cannot answer those questions merely by placing PHP, GitLab, LiteLLM, Railway, certificate-authority failures, and government warnings in the same narrative. @fiveoutofnine\nOther News\nFlint and Automated Research: Local Coding Models, Smaller Teams and Scientific Workflows\nFlint launches with broad local-serving support : Recursive released an Apache-2.0 coding MoE with roughly 40B total parameters, 2.5B active parameters and a 262K-token context window. LM Studio says it requires approximately 24GB locally. Recursive positions it as an on-device complement to—not a replacement for—frontier cloud agents ( launch , specifications ).\nClaimed results : Recursive reports 54.9% on Terminal-Bench 2.1, 89.7% on SWE-bench Pro v2 and 74.2% on SWE-bench Verified. These are release claims, not independent replication ( results ).\nPerformance : Advertised short-context speeds exceed 125 tokens/s on a MacBook Pro and 150 tokens/s on RTX Spark. A separate user measured 114–132 output tokens/s on an M5 Max with int4 MLX; that is useful corroboration of speed, not a controlled capability evaluation ( local measurement ).\nResearch contribution : Recursive says its Eureka Machine participated across ideation, implementation and validation, contributing sparse architecture choices, low-bit training and kernels. It claims a 71% size reduction with almost no quality loss and development from scratch in under four months ( method ).\nAvailability : Day-zero integrations span local frameworks and hosted access. vLLM reports 500 tokens/s on one GB200 at 100K context using NVFP4 without speculative decoding; OpenRouter offers free launch access ( vLLM , OpenRouter ).\nMirendil claims one-researcher pretraining at scale : Mirendil says its first base model was built by one researcher in 3.5 months for less than $10M in compute, including research experiments and the final run. It discloses unusually broad parameter ranges—100B–500B for Base, 500B–2T for Large and 2T–10T for XL—rather than precise configurations. Independent evaluations, post-training results and API access are still forthcoming, so this is a consequential efficiency claim awaiting external validation ( announcement ).\nJOLT jointly trains the teacher and student : Perplexity and NVIDIA’s post-training work addresses a weakness in self-distillation: a privileged teacher can exploit shortcuts unavailable to its student. JOLT trains the teacher using outcome rewards while penalizing excessive divergence from the student. The authors report matching GRPO on MATH with 6.5× fewer training tokens; JOLT+, which adds student outcome rewards, beats GRPO across five evaluated benchmarks ( method and results , benchmark summary ).\nGPT-Rosalind generates testable biological hypotheses : OpenAI reports thousands of agents linking gene-expression results from millions of mouse-brain cells with literature in 32 minutes. One proposed mechanism connects DCTN1 disruption to impaired cholesterol transport; another examines differences between TSC1 and TSC2 disruption in cerebellar neurons. These are hypotheses for experimental follow-up—not demonstrated treatments or validated drug targets ( DCTN1 work , TSC2 follow-up ).\nInference Systems: DeepInfer Economics, Native TPU Support and Power-Aware Capacity\nDeepInfer exposes production scheduling techniques : DeepSeek’s released framework combines speculative decoding, dynamic prefill sizing, decode balancing by request count and KV length, and overlapping CPU scheduling with GPU execution. A particularly reusable idea is adjusting speculative verification length using predicted draft acceptance and measured hardware throughput, making batches more regular instead of allowing slow replicas to determine completion time ( technical analysis ).\nCache accounting : A deployment-report summary cites 1.5M input tokens/s per prefill accelerator including cache hits , versus 60K excluding hits at a 96% cache-hit rate; decode is reported at 14K output tokens/s per accelerator. These are materially different throughput quantities ( report summary ).\nEconomics caveat : The same summary describes approximately $38 per accelerator-hour as revenue , not profit. Server, electricity, cooling and staffing costs remain outside that figure.\nIndependent benchmarking remains contested : SemiAnalysis says DeepInfer is absent from InferenceX because essential serving components are missing and reconstructing them would require too many assumptions. Its subsequent profitability comparison drew criticism for mixing hardware generations, peak versus off-peak prices, and revenue versus profit. The criticism is itself an attributed analysis, not a settled replication result; the practical requirement is matched hardware, workload, cache assumptions, SLOs and pricing before comparing economics ( benchmark limitation , comparison critique ).\nTorchTPU opens a native PyTorch path : Google open-sourced TorchTPU for training and serving through familiar PyTorch tooling. SGLang’s plugin is reportedly powering production inference at Meta and supports several current open models; its custom MegaKernel reaches a claimed 770 decode tokens/s for Muse Spark Open at batch size one on an 8K-input/1K-output workload. vLLM-TorchTPU adds prefix caching, speculative decoding and disaggregated serving ( Google , SGLang , vLLM ).\nGTC emphasizes deployment speed and power utilization : NVIDIA’s DSX POD packages AI infrastructure into containerized building blocks, with coverage claiming capacity expansion in days rather than months. More concretely, Lambda demonstrated 19 HGX B200 nodes operating within the power budget of 16 using DSX MaxLPS. Treat deployment timelines as product claims and the node result as a proof of concept, not fleet-wide capacity already delivered ( DSX POD , Lambda demonstration ).\nEvaluation Integrity and Agent Engineering: Measure the Harness, Not Just the Model\nMedical reasoning exposes completeness–accuracy tradeoffs : Artificial Analysis revised MLCR-AA’s prompts, reference answers and three-model judging panel. Sonnet 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra tie at 41.7%, with no model exceeding 42% under the combined completeness-and-accuracy requirement. Claude leads completeness; OpenAI leads record-grounded accuracy. Revised scores are not directly comparable with the previous methodology ( results , methodology ).\nLength confound : A cross-benchmark analysis finds Claude answers approximately 4–6× longer than OpenAI’s; each added claim creates another opportunity to contradict the record. Raw rubric scores, completeness and length-adjusted quality therefore produce different rankings ( comparison ).\nSWE-bench Multimodal faces an ablation challenge : An independent investigation argues that v2 does not meaningfully require images; a separate researcher highlights running the benchmark without images as the essential sanity check. This is a substantive critique of what the benchmark measures, not proof that its underlying coding tasks lack value ( investigation , ablation discussion ).\nEvolved harnesses and interfaces improve fixed executors : Two papers strengthen the case for optimizing the environment around agents.\nOO-Harness : Specialized subclasses, execution-derived design lessons and routing raise AutomationBench from 61.6% to 77.4% with the same Gemini 3.5 Flash executor; Workspace-Bench rises from 25.5% to 39.5% ( paper summary )."
