      "fullContent": "X 上的 Rohan Paul：“National Security Agency (NSA) and world leaders warn of security flaws found in PHP programming language\nCalif researchers first found this flaw using OpenAI’s GPT-5.6 Sol\nIn Europe, Asia and on other continents, authorities have taken a number of government websites offline for… / X\n发帖\n登录 注册\n发帖\n登录 注册\nX 上的 Rohan Paul：“National Security Agency (NSA) and world leaders warn of security flaws found in PHP programming language\nCalif researchers first found this flaw using OpenAI’s GPT-5.6 Sol\nIn Europe, Asia and on other continents, authorities have taken a number of government websites offline for the time being to protect them from newly identified security vulnerabilities in a programming language that powers the vast majority of websites online.\nThe NSA told organizations to patch 2 critical PHP flaws, dubbed PHP2Shell, or pull unpatched servers offline.\nBoth bugs live in PHP’s core, so every installation serving web requests is at risk, whatever site runs on top.\nWhen a request exhausts memory or hits a fatal error, PHP can leave half-built or freed memory that cleanup code or later requests trust.\nOn Apache’s mod_php, a demonstrated exploit triggers the corruption before any application code runs, so logins and app-level checks cannot block it.\nCloudflare saw attacks before PHP shipped fixed versions 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36 and 8.2.35 on October 21.\nVirusTotal counts about 5.1M IP addresses running PHP, and about 758K that show a version run end-of-life branches with no patched release.\nThat long tail will set how long PHP2Shell stays exploitable, since each owner must upgrade, find a backport or take the server offline.”\nRohan Paul\n@rohanpaul_ai\n显示翻译\nNational Security Agency (NSA) and world leaders warn of security flaws found in PHP programming language\nCalif researchers first found this flaw using OpenAI’s GPT-5.6 Sol\nIn Europe, Asia and on other continents, authorities have taken a number of government websites offline for the time being to protect them from newly identified security vulnerabilities in a programming language that powers the vast majority of websites online.\nThe NSA told organizations to patch 2 critical PHP flaws, dubbed PHP2Shell, or pull unpatched servers offline.\nBoth bugs live in PHP’s core, so every installation serving web requests is at risk, whatever site runs on top.\nWhen a request exhausts memory or hits a fatal error, PHP can leave half-built or freed memory that cleanup code or later requests trust.\nOn Apache’s mod_php, a demonstrated exploit triggers the corruption before any application code runs, so logins and app-level checks cannot block it.\nCloudflare saw attacks before PHP shipped fixed versions 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36 and 8.2.35 on October 21.\nVirusTotal counts about 5.1M IP addresses running PHP, and about 758K that show a version run end-of-life branches with no patched release.\nThat long tail will set how long PHP2Shell stays exploitable, since each owner must upgrade, find a backport or take the server offline.\nRohan Paul\n@rohanpaul_ai\n10月21日\nResearchers using GPT-5.6 Sol found massively critical flaw in PHP, which runs about 70% of websites, knocked major sites offline as operators rushed to patch.\nCalif, an AI security firm, who found this, said in a statement that the vulnerabilities could put 70% of the world’s\n5:13 · 2026年10月22日 · 6,325\n浏览量\n10\n10\n38\n5\nRohan Paul\n@rohanpaul_ai\n显示翻译\nNational Security Agency (NSA) and world leaders warn of security flaws found in PHP programming language\nCalif researchers first found this flaw using OpenAI’s GPT-5.6 Sol\nIn Europe, Asia and on other continents, authorities have taken a number of government websites offline for the time being to protect them from newly identified security vulnerabilities in a programming language that powers the vast majority of websites online.\nThe NSA told organizations to patch 2 critical PHP flaws, dubbed PHP2Shell, or pull unpatched servers offline.\nBoth bugs live in PHP’s core, so every installation serving web requests is at risk, whatever site runs on top.\nWhen a request exhausts memory or hits a fatal error, PHP can leave half-built or freed memory that cleanup code or later requests trust.\nOn Apache’s mod_php, a demonstrated exploit triggers the corruption before any application code runs, so logins and app-level checks cannot block it.\nCloudflare saw attacks before PHP shipped fixed versions 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36 and 8.2.35 on October 21.\nVirusTotal counts about 5.1M IP addresses running PHP, and about 758K that show a version run end-of-life branches with no patched release.\nThat long tail will set how long PHP2Shell stays exploitable, since each owner must upgrade, find a backport or take the server offline.\nRohan Paul\n@rohanpaul_ai\n10月21日\nResearchers using GPT-5.6 Sol found massively critical flaw in PHP, which runs about 70% of websites, knocked major sites offline as operators rushed to patch.\nCalif, an AI security firm, who found this, said in a statement that the vulnerabilities could put 70% of the world’s\n5:13 · 2026年10月22日 · 6,325\n浏览量\n10\n10\n38\n5\nRohan Paul\n@rohanpaul_ai\n10月22日\nNSA Advisory: Critical Patches Recommended for PHP Vulnerabilities\n自 nsa.gov\n2\n704\nSughu\n@sughanthans1\n10月22日\n🚨\nSughu\n@sughanthans1\n10月22日\nopenai and google models both helped find php2shell, the php bug that wiz says hits every php version from the last decade."
