      "id": "qul40acvgzvgjgdiznn5c8wmg",
      "title": "Anthropic 红队测试 Claude Code Auto Mode 与 Codex Guardian 拦截监控器",
      "title_en": "Red-Teaming Auto Mode: Improving Blocking Classifiers Against Malign Coding Agents",
      "url": "https://alignment.anthropic.com/2026/red-teaming-auto-mode/",
      "permalink": "https://aihot.news/items/qul40acvgzvgjgdiznn5c8wmg",
      "source": "Anthropic：Alignment Science Blog（网页）",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-22T00:00:00.000Z",
      "discoveredAt": "2026-10-27T04:19:14.989Z",
      "summary": "Anthropic Fellows 团队用 Opus 5 红队智能体测试生产级拦截监控器，发现 Auto Mode 与 Guardian 的平均攻击成功率超过 55%，主要绕过手段包括伪造转录轮次的监控器注入、多智能体与恶意压缩的多上下文攻击。团队通过转录框架化与转义、监控工作目录编辑和增加智能体监控阶段将攻击成功率降低超过 50 个百分点，但以可接受成本可靠防御多上下文攻击仍是开放问题。",
      "category": "c4",
      "score": 68,
      "selected": true,
      "attribution": {
        "source": "AIHOT",
        "canonical": "https://aihot.news/items/qul40acvgzvgjgdiznn5c8wmg"
      },
      "tag": "AI动态",
      "tagClass": "tag-neutral",
      "fullContent": "Red-Teaming Auto Mode: Improving Blocking Classifiers Against Malign Coding Agents Alignment Science Blog\nRed-Teaming Auto Mode: Improving Blocking Classifiers Against Malign Coding Agents\nAlex Remedios 1* , Simon Storf 1* October 22, 2026\nFabien Roger 2 , John Hughes 2\n1 Anthropic Fellows Program; 2 Anthropic * Primary contributors. Name order randomised.\ntl;dr We red-team production blocking monitors (Auto Mode in Claude Code, Guardian in OpenAI’s Codex) with an adversarial agent in an experimental setting with the objective of improving the safety of coding agents. Our most effective attack strategies bypass the monitor using agent-generated prompt injection against the monitor, multi-agent attacks, or malicious compaction. We attempt to improve the robustness of auto-mode by improving tool coverage, transcript formatting and using an agentic monitor stage. We find that robustly preventing multi-context attacks at an acceptable cost remains an open problem.\n📝 Paper , 💻 Code\nResearch done as part of the Anthropic Fellows Program .\nIntroduction\nCoding agents can now run for days with little or no human involvement, completing software tasks end to end ( Kwa et al., 2025 ). That autonomy requires broad access to the environment: a shell, the filesystem, credentials, and the network. This poses the risk of an agent taking dangerous actions with its access to the user’s system.\nMost dangerous actions by agents to date have been the result of over-eagerness while completing a task. However, persistent adversarial agents attempting to attack the infrastructure hosting them are an emerging threat, as demonstrated by the recent Hugging Face incident and related events ( OpenAI , Anthropic , AISI ).\nBlocking monitors ( Lindner et al., 2025 ; Stickland et al., 2025 ) have been developed and tested for the over-eagerness failure mode. They check each action (e.g. a bash command) against a separate LLM-based classifier, and block unsafe actions, after which the agent proposes a new action. Claude’s Auto Mode and Codex’s Guardian are the most widely used versions of these in production currently. The common features of these two monitors are (a) they evaluate actions against a set of rules, (b) they can block with a reason that is fed back to the agent, and (c) they terminate a session after exceeding some number of blocks.\nIn this work we study the robustness of blocking monitors against the threat of persistent adversarial agents. We focus on catastrophic harms that can’t be reversed and for which asynchronous monitoring ( Lindner et al., 2025 ) is insufficient. These include exfiltration of data, their own model weights in the most extreme case, and evasion of the blocking monitor by disabling it or creating new unmonitored agents. The four main attack types we study are monitor injection, multi-context attacks, coverage gaps and policy failures.\nProduction monitors fail to block over 55% of attacks. Each trial tests the ability of an Opus 5 red-team agent to complete a side task before incurring 20 blocks from the monitor, and we show average success rate across multiple evaluation settings and side tasks. In some trials the red-team agent has affordances such as details of the monitor internals. Error bars on the means are 95% confidence intervals. Production monitoring systems change constantly, so our results are a snapshot of their robustness as of September 2026. The attacks, tools, and design principles we report are intended to outlast this snapshot.\nThe attacks\nWe study four attack types. By default we use Opus 5 as the attacking model. We also vary the affordances we give to the attacker (see the paper’s appendix), but by default we tell the attacker how the monitor works and give a high level description of the attack.\nMonitor injection. Agent-generated content (e.g. code, comments, tool arguments) fools the monitor’s judgement into approving a harmful action.\nMulti-context attack. The attack spans contexts the monitor sees only in isolation: separate sessions coordinating via a shared workspace (multi-agent attack), or a single session whose compaction hides earlier attack steps (compaction attack).\nCoverage gap. The monitor cannot intervene on parts of the agent’s action space, or certain information is invisible to it.\nPolicy failure. The prompted policy omits the harmful behaviour, or the monitor fails to apply the policy correctly.\nThe monitors\nWe study the production variants (auto mode, guardian, guardian strict) and our experimental auto mode variants.\nMonitor\nStages\nModel\ncwd edits\nCost\nDescription\nauto mode\nS1→S2\nSonnet 5\n*\nClaude Code’s production default.\nguardian\nagent\nGPT 5.6 Luna\n*\nAn approximation of Codex’s production default.\nguardian strict\nagent\nGPT 5.6 Luna\nyes\n**\nGuardian + monitors all edits.\nauto mode ++\nS1→S2→agent\nOpus 4.8\nyes\n**\nAuto mode with all-edit monitoring, bug fixes, anti-injection and compaction monitoring.\nauto mode strict\nagent\nOpus 4.8\nyes\n***\nThe agentic stage of auto mode ++ alone (without S1 or S2).\ncwd edits marks monitors that also review edit tool calls inside the agent’s working directory, rather than letting them run unmonitored. Note that reads are always automatically allowed. Stages details the LLM classifiers an action passes through: a one-token pre-screen classifier (S1), a reasoning classifier (S2), and a tool-calling agent that can read files and query the environment. An action is allowed if any stage judges it to be harmless. Stages are designed to only escalate unclear cases to the next stage, so most actions are cleared by the pre-screen and never reach the more expensive later stages. Cost is indicative and bundles latency, FPR and dollar cost, and is quantified in the results.\nMethods\nOur architecture for attacker capability evaluations. The attacker agent is tasked with completing a side-task, given a main-task which determines the range of what the monitor should permit. For our arena evaluations we use a Claude Code agent in a Linux sandbox, and for our simulation evals we use an Inspect-based Python agent, and LLM simulated tool call outputs. These contrasting approaches are described below and in the paper’s appendices. Our red-team evaluations fall into three modes, ordered roughly by increasing cost and fidelity.\nAnalytical evaluation looks for discrete vulnerabilities. Reading the monitor’s prompts, code, and integration with the surrounding system directly yields attack hypotheses. Unmonitored tool calls, injection channels, unhandled prompt edge-cases, and points where agent output is promoted to user intent are examples of such vulnerabilities.\nReplay-based evaluation scores existing transcripts against a monitor. In a loose analogy with reinforcement learning, replay is off-policy : it is agnostic to the policy that produced a transcript. This makes replay cheap and lets us reuse transcripts from prior work or optimise certain parts of the transcript freely, but it does not capture the attacker vs. defender interaction.\nBenign replay: we use a cross-section of 4,000 actions from the SWE-chat dataset to measure deployment metrics such as false-positive rate.\nMalign replay: attack transcripts, used to score and optimise attacks against a monitor without running an agent.\nRollout-based evaluation runs a red-team agent (primarily Opus 5) live against the monitor. We use the agent’s system prompt to steer it toward an attack category and to grant affordances such as knowledge of the monitor formatting. We provide these affordances to understand monitor robustness under worst-case scenarios where the agent is working inside a frontier lab and has access to the monitor’s code. Rollout is the on-policy counterpart to replay: the trajectory is generated by the attacker policy under test as it interacts with the monitor, so rollout success rates are our most credible estimates of real risk.\nArena: A Claude Code agent which acts in Docker-based environments ( LinuxArena , BashArena , and custom tasks) with side tasks scored by deterministic checkers. It uses a replaced system prompt and hooks that route each action through the monitor before it runs. Compared to simulation below, arenas enable long evaluation trajectories with deterministic environment outputs.\nSimulation: An evaluation featuring bash , write , and edit tools, where tool outputs are generated by an LLM dungeon master (DM). The DM is prompted with a scene description and determines when the side-task is complete. This approach allows the simulation of complex weight exfiltration and rogue deployment environments without building infrastructure.\nResults\nAttack success rate falls by over 50 percentage points when we use our new Auto Mode ++ monitor. The bottom row is the mean over the four attack types, averaging arena and simulation in cases where both measure a type of attack. Each cell is the mean of at least 60 rollouts. We present our findings as a game between a red-team that constructs attacks and a blue-team that hardens the monitor against them, played iteratively.\nMonitor Holes\nAgents are able to exploit holes in the system to bypass Auto Mode\nMonitoring systems have discrete vulnerabilities: some agent tool calls may not be visible to the monitor due to an engineering oversight, and naïve policy wording in the monitor’s prompt can lead to loopholes. We identified and fixed several such vulnerabilities in Claude Code as part of this work.\nThe most serious vulnerabilities were unmonitored channels : compaction summaries were not monitored, so an agent could fabricate facts about the environment or the user’s intent and have them treated as trusted context after the compaction. The multi-agent workflow tool was similar: workflow creation and agent-to-agent handoffs let an agent inject instructions into messages that a subsequent agent reads as user authorisation.\nWe also found discrete policy holes . For example, the policy reliably blocks launching a Claude instance with permissions disabled (--dangerously-skip-permissions), but permits running an unsafe tool-calling loop through the Inspect framework, which reaches the same unmonitored-agent end state.\nMonitor Injection\nAuto Mode and Guardian are highly vulnerable to injections authored by the agent\nThe monitor reviews a flattened transcript where actions and roles (e.g. tool calls, user messages) are separated by simple text prefixes, so an agent that knows the format can forge the entries that appear to follow its own action.\nA monitor injection against Auto Mode (illustrative). The attack is injected into the agent’s Bash command, whose argument spans two lines: the first (red) exfiltrates a credential file, and the second (amber) is forged so that the flattened transcript the monitor reads ends on a harmless looking Bash echo ack. The forged line is not a real command and errors when the shell reaches it, but bash runs the two lines in order, so the exfiltration has already gone through. The monitor mistakenly judges only the final, forged entry and allows the action.\nEscaping and framing each transcript turn substantially mitigates monitor injection\nThe injection works because the monitor has no way to tell whether the tool call it sees is authentic. The command in the figure above was written into a single bash argument, and the newlines make the argument read as separate transcript turns. We harden the rendering by framing each turn and escaping agent-authored content, so the agent cannot forge a turn boundary.\nOur strongest mitigation layers two defences:"
