      "fullContent": "X 上的 Rohan Paul：“Researchers using GPT-5.6 Sol found massively critical flaw in PHP, which runs about 70% of websites, knocked major sites offline as operators rushed to patch.\nCalif, an AI security firm, who found this, said in a statement that the vulnerabilities could put 70% of the world’s web… / X\n发帖\n登录 注册\n发帖\n登录 注册\nX 上的 Rohan Paul：“Researchers using GPT-5.6 Sol found massively critical flaw in PHP, which runs about 70% of websites, knocked major sites offline as operators rushed to patch.\nCalif, an AI security firm, who found this, said in a statement that the vulnerabilities could put 70% of the world’s websites at risk if they aren’t patched quickly, and called them “the most severe vulnerabilities ever discovered by AI, if not in all of software history.”\n> PHP rushed out fixes as it could let attackers without accounts take over web servers.\n> Both bugs score 9.2 on the CVSS severity scale and affect PHP versions as old as 5.4.\n> When a request hits PHP’s memory cap at the wrong moment, PHP frees memory but keeps pointers to it, which a later request on that worker can hijack.\n> Auditors spotted the weakness in 2024, but the fix stalled for months, partly because nobody had shown a practical attack.\n> Wiz, a cloud security company, now owned by Google, says the models let one researcher finish in a week what once took a top team months.\n> A leak of the CVE numbers forced PHP to ship a day early, and Cloudflare now reports early attack attempts.”\nRohan Paul\n@rohanpaul_ai\n显示翻译\nResearchers using GPT-5.6 Sol found massively critical flaw in PHP, which runs about 70% of websites, knocked major sites offline as operators rushed to patch.\nCalif, an AI security firm, who found this, said in a statement that the vulnerabilities could put 70% of the world’s websites at risk if they aren’t patched quickly, and called them “the most severe vulnerabilities ever discovered by AI, if not in all of software history.”\n> PHP rushed out fixes as it could let attackers without accounts take over web servers.\n> Both bugs score 9.2 on the CVSS severity scale and affect PHP versions as old as 5.4.\n> When a request hits PHP’s memory cap at the wrong moment, PHP frees memory but keeps pointers to it, which a later request on that worker can hijack.\n> Auditors spotted the weakness in 2024, but the fix stalled for months, partly because nobody had shown a practical attack.\n> Wiz, a cloud security company, now owned by Google, says the models let one researcher finish in a week what once took a top team months.\n> A leak of the CVE numbers forced PHP to ship a day early, and Cloudflare now reports early attack attempts.\n17:40 · 2026年10月21日 · 9,567\n浏览量\n9\n16\n59\n14\nRohan Paul\n@rohanpaul_ai\n显示翻译\nResearchers using GPT-5.6 Sol found massively critical flaw in PHP, which runs about 70% of websites, knocked major sites offline as operators rushed to patch.\nCalif, an AI security firm, who found this, said in a statement that the vulnerabilities could put 70% of the world’s websites at risk if they aren’t patched quickly, and called them “the most severe vulnerabilities ever discovered by AI, if not in all of software history.”\n> PHP rushed out fixes as it could let attackers without accounts take over web servers.\n> Both bugs score 9.2 on the CVSS severity scale and affect PHP versions as old as 5.4.\n> When a request hits PHP’s memory cap at the wrong moment, PHP frees memory but keeps pointers to it, which a later request on that worker can hijack.\n> Auditors spotted the weakness in 2024, but the fix stalled for months, partly because nobody had shown a practical attack.\n> Wiz, a cloud security company, now owned by Google, says the models let one researcher finish in a week what once took a top team months.\n> A leak of the CVE numbers forced PHP to ship a day early, and Cloudflare now reports early attack attempts.\n17:40 · 2026年10月21日 · 9,567\n浏览量\n9\n16\n59\n14\nRohan Paul\n@rohanpaul_ai\n10月21日\nPatch the Planet: global response coordination as agents uncover critical vulnerabilities\n自 openai.com\n3\n697\nRohan Paul\n@rohanpaul_ai\n10月22日\nRohan Paul\n@rohanpaul_ai\n10月22日\nNational Security Agency (NSA) and world leaders warn of security flaws found in PHP programming language\nCalif researchers first found this flaw using OpenAI’s GPT-5.6 Sol\nIn Europe, Asia and on other continents, authorities have taken a number of government websites offline\n2\n790\nRasa\n@Abel198429\n10月21日\nSaid goodbye to PHP a while back, I am good but thanks\n1\n76"
